@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# Audio seek failure and cancellation
+
+A seek stops the old audio source before creating and starting a replacement. If replacement connection or startup throws, the old playback cannot continue. Previously the stopped playback remained globally active, its context remained open, and the event handler surfaced an uncaught exception rather than the existing playback error message.
+
+The seek path now disconnects a replacement that fails setup, releases the active playback and context, and rethrows the original error. The player catches it, resets its state and uses the existing localized playback-failure message. A subsequent play can retry. This also covers a failure in the progress callback or animation scheduling after replacement. Teardown is best-effort and does not guarantee an operating-system audio handle closes if the platform close itself fails.
+
+A cancelled pointer drag also restores both the rendered progress and the synchronous progress reference. Without that, a keyboard event in the same React batch could seek from the cancelled preview rather than the restored position. End and reset paths likewise update the reference synchronously.
+
+Tests reproduce both faults and cover connect/start failures, context and node cleanup, a real utility failure through the player, retry, and pointer cancellation followed immediately by keyboard input. They use mocked Web Audio and jsdom; hardware and platform-specific behavior remains unverified.
+
+The audio cache previously checked its 60-second age only on lookup, so expired idle samples remained retained. It now schedules eviction after successful admission and cancels timers on invalidation or eviction; a stale timer cannot remove a replacement entry. The age check remains a fallback when a timer is delayed. Browser timer throttling or suspension can delay eviction, and other consumers can retain their own references, so this is not a hard wall-clock or zeroization guarantee. Explicit local-data clear still invalidates the cache and its timers.
+
+## Waiting for the previous audio context
+
+An unresolved `AudioContext.close()` previously left subsequent playback waiting indefinitely, even after cancellation. Waiting now observes cancellation and has a five-second timeout, reported through the existing playback error path. A timed-out close remains tracked so retries cannot allocate more contexts while it is still pending; once it settles a retry can proceed. Waiters remove their listeners and timeout registrations on settlement, cancellation, or timeout. Synchronous close exceptions are treated like the existing rejected-close path so they do not suppress the owner callback or mask a setup error. This remains best-effort: a rejected or synchronously throwing close is not proof the OS resource was released, and an unowned context closed during failed setup is not part of the shared close tracker. Tests use mocked Web Audio, not hardware.
