+# Authorization error redaction
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+Provider errors and desktop diagnostics may contain authorization headers. At the consolidation baseline `8dd1f8735d8a038957046bad6315bdf82f04e8ac`, both `packages/utilities/src/error.ts` and `packages/voice-ai/src/provider-error.utils.ts` treated only a fixed set of scheme names as syntax. A custom scheme caused each redactor to remove the scheme and retain the credential. A second header on the same line could also escape redaction.
+
+HTTP defines an authentication scheme as a case-insensitive token, not a closed list. See [RFC 9110 section 11.1](https://github.com/httpwg/http-core/blob/main/rfc9110.xml#L5835-L5844) and its [token grammar](https://github.com/httpwg/http-core/blob/main/rfc9110.xml#L1845-L1856).
+
+Both redactors now treat a token followed by a credential as a possible scheme. This can remove a word of diagnostic prose after a bare value. That loss is preferable to logging a credential. When a quote that starts a credential never closes, the redactors now remove the remaining credential text instead of leaving it in the clear. The previous utilities test expected the tail of that unterminated value to remain visible. That expectation exposed credential text and was corrected.
+
+The shared scheme scanner also accepts `=` assignments and underscore-qualified authorization labels. Before this change, `authorization=Digest nonce="...", response="..."` and `x_authorization: Digest ...` hid the first parameter but exposed later ones. Focused tests reproduced both leaks, and the provider scanner is tested on the same separator and label shapes.
+
+The utilities scanner resumes at the credential boundary. It does not search for the next header inside a quoted credential. It caches the line end so multiple headers do not repeatedly scan the whole remaining line. The provider scanner processes authorization labels before generic bearer patterns. Otherwise an empty bearer header can cause the generic pattern to consume the next header name and leave its credential behind.
+
+A later provider-domain audit found that an opaque key in a JSON `api_key` field was redacted by deleting the key and separator, producing invalid JSON. Quoted API-key labels, including common qualified labels, are now retained like quoted authorization labels. Non-string values under either label are replaced as complete values: previously numeric values produced invalid JSON, and nested objects or arrays could expose their contents. A single-pass container scan honors nested brackets and quoted escapes; an unterminated container is withheld through the end of the message. Free-text labels keep their existing label-erasing behavior. Regression tests cover valid JSON, siblings, nested values and malformed input. The qualified-key preservation recognizes ASCII letters, digits, spaces, dots, underscores and hyphens; it is not a general JSON parser.
+
+A further audit found that an unterminated quoted value after an `api_key`, `password` or bare authorization label exposed its tail. The shared scanner now handles quoted field values directly and redacts an unterminated value through the line end. The provider scanner applies the same fallback to API-key fields and accepts quoted values that start outside its unquoted token character set. The legacy provider equivalence test retains exact comparisons for closed values. For unterminated values it checks that the new output reveals no more than the old output, because exact equality would preserve the leak.
+
+The free-form pass must run before quoted-field redaction. A trial that scanned quoted fields first exposed the tail of `password: firstPart api_key: "nestedValue" lastPart`, because the nested field split the enclosing passphrase. A regression test now covers that ordering. The existing free-form label pattern can take superlinear time on long runs of holder words. In this Arena sandbox, an unterminated quoted value with 20,000 repetitions of `secret ` took about 3.7 seconds. The faster ordering was rejected because it changed the confidentiality boundary. The performance case remains open.
+
+A separate failure path could bypass structural redaction: a throwing property getter caused `unknownToMessage` to call the original object's `toString`, which could return an unlabelled password. A `toJSON` that returned `undefined` reached the same fallback. Both cases now return a redaction marker instead; throwing Error message getters and revoked proxies also fail closed. Primitive values retain their string fallback.
+
+Another regression test reproduced a mutable `toJSON` getter that appeared only after the initial serializer check, causing the copied method to run after redaction and emit the original password. Callable values are no longer copied into the sanitized result. Object copies have a null prototype so an own `__proto__` is data, not a prototype setter. Array copies also avoid calling an input array's `map` or species constructor: both can return an unredacted result. Sparse arrays keep their JSON representation.
+
+Regression tests cover custom token characters, consecutive headers, quoted values, unterminated values and a long line containing 10,000 headers. The provider and utilities suites also retain their existing tests for known schemes and surrounding diagnostics.
+
+### Empty fields followed by another credential
+
+A header dump can contain an empty credential field immediately before another
+credential, including an empty Bearer scheme. Previously the first field could
+consume the second field's name as its value, leaving the second value visible.
+Both text scrubbers now recognize a following credential label before consuming
+it; the shared scrubber uses its existing label vocabulary, while the provider
+scrubber uses its narrower API-key and authorization vocabulary. The bare
+Bearer pass also avoids swallowing a following recognized label while retaining
+support for a credential on the following line. Regression tests cover empty,
+scheme-only, repeated, and quoted fields. This does not expand either scrubber's
+recognized-label vocabulary or resolve the broader performance and trust limits
+noted above.
+
+### Bounded diagnostic rendering
+
+`unknownToMessage` now applies a shared 16,384-unit inspection budget, a 1,024-entry collection limit, and a 1,024-character object-key limit before expensive string scans and before reading values of oversized arrays or objects. Exceeding a limit withholds the entire diagnostic as `[redacted]`, rather than returning an unsafe prefix. Parsed JSON that exceeds the budget does not fall back to the less expressive text scrubber, and budget exhaustion inside `toJSON` propagates to the same fail-closed result. This deliberately sacrifices some diagnostics longer than the final 512-character output cap.
+
+This limits ordinary oversized inputs to that entry point, not arbitrary hostile JavaScript execution: object key enumeration, proxies, getters and `toJSON` can themselves run arbitrary work. The separately exported `redactSensitiveTokens` remains uncapped and its free-form pattern can still be superlinear; no claim of a generally linear redactor is made.
