+# Auto-learn privacy transitions
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+A transcript correction can await a repository write before starting its glossary writes. Previously, if incognito mode or an ephemeral session started during that await, the saved initial decision still allowed the glossary write. Regression tests reproduce both transitions. The action now requires persistence to have been allowed at the beginning **and** to be allowed immediately before starting auto-learn. A correction that began in a privacy mode still cannot acquire permission merely because the mode is turned off.
+
+Learned terms are now submitted one at a time. The action rechecks privacy before each term, and the dictionary queue checks it again immediately before the repository call; a skipped optimistic term is removed and reported as suppressed rather than failed. This covers a transition during an earlier term and one occurring while the next term is queued. The dialog reports partial additions, failures and suppression together and describes both supported privacy modes rather than incorrectly attributing every suppression to Incognito. Feedback messages are translated in every supported locale; their source translations are fed through the catalog generator.
+
+This remains a point-in-time check, not an atomic privacy barrier. It cannot undo a transcript or glossary write already in progress or detect a mode enabled and disabled between checks. A stronger guarantee requires a shared privacy epoch or cancellation protocol across all persistence paths and windows, with native-side coordination where needed; this focused change does not claim that guarantee.
+
+The audit also found that an unexpected glossary exception after a successful transcript update restored only the in-memory transcript, leaving the saved row different. The action now retains the completed correction and reports that learning could not be completed. Dictionary creation no longer treats a post-insert callback exception as a database insertion failure; it retains the persisted lane and propagates the exception instead of removing an already-saved term.
+
+Concurrent corrections to the same transcript previously reached the repository together. A later failure could restore an older snapshot over a newer successful correction. Corrections now run in per-transcript request order, while different transcripts remain independent. Privacy permission is captured before entering the queue and checked again after it, so a request begun in privacy mode cannot become persistent while waiting. When another action removes or replaces the optimistic row during the repository call, the correction no longer restores or overwrites it in the store, and it does not start glossary learning for that stale row.
+
+This lane only orders correction actions; it does not serialize deletion, retranscription or other repository writers. In particular, it does not resolve database-level races with those paths, and a deletion after glossary learning has already begun does not cancel the in-flight write. That broader coordination remains separate work.
