index 00000000..e62dc920
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/cask-publication-safety.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Homebrew cask publication safeguards
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The tap publication must fetch its existing `main` before writing a cask. A failed fetch is no longer treated as an empty tap, and an unchanged staged tree is distinguished from a failed commit. Fetch, commit and push errors now fail the job. Regression tests execute the workflow shell against a temporary local bare repository, including rejected commits and pushes.
+
+The renderer also refuses a template without exactly one recognized version, checksum and release-tag placeholder in the expected download URL. This prevents a template change from silently producing a cask with an old value or missing checksum. Template format changes must update the renderer deliberately.
+
+These checks do not make release and cask publication atomic: the GitHub release is published first, so a later tap failure leaves a published release with an outdated cask and requires operator recovery. Local Git fixtures do not exercise GitHub authentication, Homebrew evaluation, or an actual release. The cask checksum is computed from the downloaded release asset and is not independent provenance for that asset.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/chat-load-and-delete-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/chat-load-and-delete-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..bd97ea85
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/chat-load-and-delete-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Chat load and delete ownership
+
+A chat list or message-history read can finish after a local mutation or a privacy clear. Previously it could restore a deleted conversation, replace a newer title, hide a newly persisted message, or restore cleared content into memory. A history load could also hide the currently streaming assistant bubble because that bubble is not necessarily in the repository yet. Concurrent deletes of one conversation could release their shared deleting flag independently.
+
+Conversation creation, update and deletion now participate in one renderer-local mutation tracker. List reads wait for tracked mutations, retry when one overlaps their read, and only the newest list request may publish. History reads retry if their local message IDs, ordinary message objects or streaming membership change during the read; a newer request supersedes an older one. Current in-memory assistant messages with streaming metadata are retained rather than overwritten by the repository result. Both read paths decline publication across a local-data clear generation or while a clear is active. A delete immediately invalidates an in-flight history read even for a deep link whose conversation was not yet loaded.
+
+Concurrent deletes of the same ID share the pending result, including failure; another delete can be attempted afterward. Deletion aborts the agent immediately, waits for the existing send queue without holding a write lease, and does not erase a newer queue registration. Successful deletion removes the deleted messages' streaming metadata and clears the pill selection only if it still points at that conversation. The chat page catches history-load failures and reports them only while that selection is current; the list-load effect consumes the rejection after the action records it.
+
+These are renderer-local protections, not database transactions or cross-process change notifications. Reads may retry repeatedly during sustained local mutation and no timeout is imposed. A streaming entry is preserved while its streaming metadata exists; a failed final persistence followed by removal of that metadata is not made durable by this change. Existing already-started native writes cannot be retracted. A send rejected by the existing deleting-conversation guard can still complete without a durable message; this work does not redesign that API or restore typed text. Repository and UI tests use mocks, not a live database, renderer restart, or simultaneous independent processes.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/chat-message-missing-update.md b/docs/decisions/chat-message-missing-update.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..72aa8603
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/chat-message-missing-update.md
@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+# Missing chat-message updates
+
+The native chat-message update previously returned a clone of the requested message even if SQLite matched no row. A concurrent deletion could therefore look like a successful save and reinsert a nonexistent message into renderer state; manual paste review could proceed on the strength of that false success. The update now returns SQLx `RowNotFound` when no row was affected, so callers receive a failure instead. A matching unchanged row remains a successful update.
+
+A Rust SQLite test reproduced the false success; tests now cover missing, changed and unchanged rows. A separate Python SQLite check also observed zero rows affected for a missing ID and one for a matching unchanged ID. Mocked renderer tests verify that a failed initial save prevents a clipboard write, and that a failed final status save is surfaced after an already-invoked clipboard write (which cannot be undone). This is not a compare-and-swap: a concurrent edit to an existing row may still be overwritten, and a row deleted after a successful update is outside this check. Native and renderer write coordination remains per process/renderer as documented elsewhere.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/consolidation-supersedes-parallel-refactor.md b/docs/decisions/consolidation-supersedes-parallel-refactor.md
