--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/dictation-backlog-session-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Dictation backlog and session ownership
+
+Realtime dictation serializes paste and backlog work within a strategy instance. Cleanup invalidates its session and replaces the queue, but an already-running promise can still settle. Reproductions showed a late failed interim paste appending old text after cleanup, an old drain changing new-session bookkeeping, an old target lookup replacing the new target, and queued old finalization draining a new backlog.
+
+The strategy now checks its session generation before applying those completions or starting a queued final drain. Target lookup also uses a request revision so an older lookup in the same session cannot overwrite a newer one. A stale finalization does not use the new session's accumulated text as its fallback.
+
+The backlog delivery utility now removes only the snapshot prefix it delivered. A separate reset generation protects a same-text replacement and prevents a second acknowledgement from removing a later identical segment. A failed delivery leaves the snapshot and appended segments in place. A new segment passed only as a drain argument is not part of the stored backlog; the existing caller remains responsible for retaining it when delivery fails.
+
+The standalone drain also declines overlapping calls for the same backlog generation before invoking delivery. It returns `delivered: false`, so a caller passing a new segment must retain that segment and retry. Failure releases the guard. A cleared or new generation can proceed while an older delivery is still pending.
+
+These are renderer-local guarantees. A clear cannot retract an already-invoked paste or typing operation, and a new generation can deliver while that old operation is still in flight. Session checks cannot undo side effects inside an already-started app-target registration. Multiple renderer processes or untracked direct store resets are not coordinated. Tests use mocked paste and target boundaries rather than real applications.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/fallow-audit-skips-the-cycle-pass.md b/docs/decisions/fallow-audit-skips-the-cycle-pass.md
