@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+# The Fallow audit skips the cycle pass; a separate gate owns cycles
+
+Date: 2026-11-06
+Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`fallow audit --gate new-only` attributes a finding to whichever file a pull
+request touched, which is the wrong unit for a *cycle*: a cycle has no home file,
+so editing any one of its members makes an inherited cycle look introduced. On
+this branch that produced five failures that the base branch had before the
+branch existed — `actions/transcribe.actions.ts` reaching the session layer,
+which reaches `transcribe.actions.ts` back through `repos`, `utils/user.utils.ts`
+and `sessions/index.ts`. The audit offered no config lever to exclude them:
+`audit.deadCode` is a boolean that also drops unused-file findings, and
+`ignoreFindings` does not cover cycles.
+
+The one cycle edge that was removable *was* removed: the pre-transcriber imported
+its metadata type from `actions/transcribe.actions.ts` and now imports it from
+`types/transcription-metadata.types.ts`.
+
+## Decision
+
+`pnpm run fallow:audit` passes `--skip architecture`, so the pull-request gate
+judges dead code, complexity, duplication and styling only. Import cycles are
+gated instead by `scripts/ci/fallow.test.mjs`, which runs the cycle analysis once
+and fails on any cycle that is not in `INHERITED_CYCLES` — an explicit list with
+a comment per entry.
+
+Rejected: performing the layer inversion (moving the transcription call out of
+`actions` into a module the session layer may import) as part of a
+pull-request-babysitting pass. It touches every file in the cycle, changes
+runtime import order in the transcription path, and its only benefit here is to
+make an inherited finding stop being *reported* as new.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A new cycle still fails CI, through `node --test scripts/ci/*.test.mjs`, and
+  the failing message names both endpoints of the cycle.
+- `fallow audit` no longer reports cycles at all, so a reviewer looking for them
+  has to run `test:fallow` or `fallow health` to see them.
+- When the cycle is genuinely broken later, the entry comes out of
+  `INHERITED_CYCLES` in the same commit that breaks it: the same test also fails
+  when a listed cycle is no longer observed, so the list cannot quietly outlive
+  the cycles it describes. Adding that assertion is how two entries left stale by
+  the metadata-type move were found and deleted.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/gemini-file-cleanup-disclosure.md b/docs/decisions/gemini-file-cleanup-disclosure.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..ec577ebd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/gemini-file-cleanup-disclosure.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Gemini Files API cleanup and diagnostics
+
+Uploaded audio is deleted on a best-effort basis after transcription or fallback. Cleanup now reports a generic warning on non-success HTTP responses as well as transport exceptions; previously an HTTP error was treated as a successful deletion. The warning and the upload-fallback warning no longer include raw URIs, provider payloads or exceptions, which may contain opaque secrets or private identifiers. Cleanup failure still does not fail an otherwise successful transcription.
+
+A generic warning does not prove a file remains: a not-found response may mean it is already gone. Conversely, no retry or independent confirmation is performed, so failed or aborted cleanup may leave provider-side audio. Resumable upload URLs are now restricted to HTTPS Google API hosts with no embedded credentials or nondefault port, and file URIs that receive the API key are restricted to the exact Gemini API host. The test-only example host is no longer trusted in production. Gemini requests ask their transport to reject redirects, since redirects could otherwise forward audio or the custom API-key header. This depends on the supplied fetch implementation honoring that option; a broad `*.googleapis.com` upload allowance is not a cryptographic proof that a destination is a particular Google service. A legitimate future provider host or redirect outside these rules will cause inline fallback or failure, and compatibility with live Gemini was not verified. Tests mock the provider and do not verify real deletion or network enforcement.
+
+Gemini non-success HTTP responses now retain their numeric status and retry hint but do not read or propagate provider error bodies. A best-effort body cancellation is initiated without waiting, so a stalled error body cannot delay the status failure. This trades detailed provider diagnostics for confidentiality; transport exceptions, successful response contents, other providers, and cleanup resource release are outside this guarantee. Cancellation depends on the fetch implementation and may not complete.
+
+When a chat consumer stops iterating, stream reader cancellation is initiated without waiting for an uncooperative transport; cancellation and lock release remain best effort. A pending `next()` or a stalled fetch still depends on transport/abort behavior, and cancellation is not guaranteed to stop the remote server.
+
+Gemini chat parsing rejects an individual SSE event or unfinished event over 4 Mi UTF-16 code units, while allowing any number of smaller events. The threshold is a defensive compatibility tradeoff, not a provider-specified maximum: exceptionally large tool arguments or output can now fail. A transport can still allocate one oversized chunk before this check, and the body-less response fallback reads the transport's entire text before checking. Repeated tiny chunks can also incur repeated buffer scans; these bounds do not solve all transport resource exhaustion.
+
+An SSE frame containing an `error` field now fails the chat stream with a generic message instead of silently producing a successful finish; any text already emitted remains visible to the consumer and cannot be retracted. JSON scalar and array frames are rejected as malformed. This does not prove a stream was complete when the transport closes without an explicit error or finish reason.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/groq-model-picker-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/groq-model-picker-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..7dae6f9d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/groq-model-picker-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+# Groq model picker ownership
+
+The model list is scoped to the credential that requested it. Changing or removing the credential unmounts the old request owner, aborts its signal, and discards late responses. Switching back to an earlier credential starts a fresh owner instead of showing an old result. A ten-second deadline covers both the request and response body; on timeout the picker shows its existing unavailable state. Request failures log only a generic diagnostic.
+
+The key is still passed to the network client as required to authenticate and remains in renderer memory. Aborting cannot guarantee that a transport or server cancels work already dispatched; the ownership check only prevents stale publication. A timeout has no automatic retry: the user can change/re-enter the credential or reopen the picker. Tests use mocked fetch rather than a live Groq endpoint.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/hotkey-and-permission-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/hotkey-and-permission-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..bd194b96
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/hotkey-and-permission-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Hotkey and agent-permission ownership
+
+A hotkey list read could overwrite an optimistic shortcut edit, resurrect shortcuts after local-data clear, or let an older load replace a newer one. The style-hotkey dialog bypassed the shared write queue and released its repository lease before publishing the committed result and syncing native shortcuts. It could overwrite an edit made while the replace was in flight or publish after a clear.
+
+Hotkey loading now waits for tracked writes, retries when a queued or optimistic write overlaps its read, and discards results across a local-data clear or a newer load. A successful read replaces the hotkey map as well as the ordered IDs, including safe own properties for unusual IDs. Style-hotkey replacement uses the shared queue, keeps a local-data write lease through store publication and native sync, and preserves later optimistic changes and deletions. If native sync fails after SQLite committed, the committed store remains visible and the dialog stays open for retry.
+
+Permission approvals previously accepted a late click after timeout and the two UI paths saved an always-allow grant before checking whether the request was still pending. A stale, aborted, expired or denied request could therefore grant later executions. Resolution now validates runtime status, timeout, clear state and ownership by an active agent call before allowing, and only an accepted `allowed` resolution can create an always-allow grant. Repeated approvals and non-boolean always-allow flags do not create grants. The chat card and overlay use the same resolution path.
+
+These protections are renderer-local. Hotkey reads or sync operations already sent to native code cannot be retracted, and waiting for a stuck queued write or sync can delay a privacy clear indefinitely. The style replacement transaction cannot undo a successful database write when native sync fails. Direct untracked shortcut mutations, other processes and a stale editor submitted after a completed replacement are not coordinated. Permission checks rely on the in-memory run/call association and wall-clock timeout; a backward clock jump can delay expiry. An already-dispatched tool cannot be recalled, and existing legacy/global always-allow grants remain honored. Tests use mocked repositories, WebView state and native sync, not real platform shortcuts or adversarial native IPC.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/import-and-streak-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/import-and-streak-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..3856ff86
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/import-and-streak-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Audio-import and streak ownership
+
+An audio import could begin before a privacy clear and finish after it, starting transcription or styling, persisting an old result, or recreating an incognito session row. Imports now capture the renderer's clear generation before opening the picker and check it and the shared pending-clear marker after the picker, after transcription, after styling, after storage and after retry scheduling. If invalidated, the import rejects rather than reporting success or creating a memory row. The shared audio pipeline also checks retranscription ownership between transcription and styling so a clear does not knowingly dispatch the next provider call.
+
+Streak decisions formerly used a profile snapshot taken before entering the whole-profile mutation queue. Overlapping recordings could save and celebrate the same streak twice, and a prior queued profile update could make the calculation stale. The queued update now computes from the latest reconciled profile and can skip an unnecessary upsert. Delayed streak work captures the clear generation when scheduled, checks again inside the mutation queue and suppresses rewards when a clear invalidates it.
+
+These guarantees are renderer-local except for the pending-clear marker. A clear that starts and completes entirely in another renderer cannot advance this renderer's generation; cross-window coordination is not a durable shared epoch. In-flight provider calls and already-dispatched native effects cannot be cancelled or recalled, nor are audio buffers securely zeroized. Storage started before a clear retains its write lease until it drains; a stuck write can delay clearing. The import error is surfaced by the existing dialog, and an interrupted picker is not forcibly closed. Streak calculation uses the local date when the queued update executes and is not a cross-process transactional compare-and-set. Native/provider behavior was mocked in regression tests.
+
+Usage-word recording also acquires or retains its local-data write lease before entering the profile queue. Previously a usage event without an existing lease could wait behind a long read until a clear had finished, then acquire a fresh lease and repopulate deleted activity. A clear now waits for already-accepted queued usage writes, so a hung profile queue can delay clearing; writes requested after the gate closes still fail.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/installer-async-file-io.md b/docs/decisions/installer-async-file-io.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..0125f38e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/installer-async-file-io.md
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# Installer download file I/O
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+SonarCloud flags the synchronous file writes and cleanup in the async macOS installer command (`apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/commands.rs`). The transfer can be hundreds of megabytes, so slow filesystem operations can indeed hold an async worker. The existing helper uses `create_new`, caps streamed bytes, closes before deleting a failed partial file, and has an incomplete-transfer cleanup test.
+
+A straightforward switch to Tokio file APIs was not made as part of this pass. Tokio runs filesystem operations on a blocking pool; cancellation during an asynchronous create can leave a newly created file after the future is dropped, and asynchronous writes need careful completion and cleanup ordering on Windows. A fix should preserve exclusive creation, size enforcement and partial-file deletion, explicitly test cancellation and worker responsiveness, and be checked on macOS and Windows rather than trading away those guarantees to silence the warning.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/legacy-database-migration.md b/docs/decisions/legacy-database-migration.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c53a3ad8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/legacy-database-migration.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Legacy database migration safety
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The old migration copied the previous-brand `voquill.db` and then copied its `-wal` and `-shm` separately while discarding sidecar errors. A local SQLite reproduction checkpointed the schema, committed one row with auto-checkpoint disabled, and copied only the main file while the connection remained open. The live source returned that row; the copy returned none, with 4,152 bytes in the WAL. An interrupted or failed copy could also leave a current main file that suppressed retry.
+
+The migration now opens the legacy database read-only and uses SQLite `VACUUM INTO` to produce a consistent standalone snapshot. It stages in a uniquely allocated quarantine-namespace directory, synchronizes the snapshot, and publishes it with no-clobber semantics. On Unix the staging directory is created with mode 0700 and the published snapshot with mode 0600. Existing current entries are left untouched. Orphaned current SQLite sidecars prevent normal startup when the current main database is missing, even if there is no legacy source; they are also rechecked immediately before publication rather than being mixed with the new snapshot. A pending clear may still attempt recovery. Source inspection, SQLite, copy, and publication errors are surfaced rather than silently starting with a partial copy. Read-only access to a live WAL still depends on SQLite and filesystem permissions; if it cannot be read, startup fails rather than omitting committed frames.
+
+Ordinary completion and errors remove the staging directory through the same no-follow, known-file cleanup used by the privacy wipe; cleanup failures are surfaced. If the process is interrupted, the staging directory can remain and may contain private data. It uses the existing quarantine naming convention so the explicit local-data clear removes it along with other quarantined databases. Cleanup refuses unexpected archive contents and links, but its provenance limitation remains: a coincidentally named user directory containing only recognized filenames, or a matching standalone file, can be removed. See `quarantine-recovery-limits.md`. A later normal startup retries from the preserved legacy source; it does not automatically remove abandoned staging directories.
+
+When native local-data clear is pending, the path resolver skips legacy import. Separate quarantine markers block legacy import after a new quarantine and fail closed after an interrupted one, with the limitations described in `quarantine-recovery-limits.md`.
+
+Tests exercise committed WAL frames with a live writer, failure before publication, destination collisions, orphan sidecars, staging cleanup during privacy clear, and the clear/quarantine recovery guards on Linux. Current-database quarantine and orphan-sidecar checks also run when no legacy directory can be resolved, and quarantine history suppresses import without suppressing the sidecar check. Pending clear recovery still bypasses those checks so the retry dialog can proceed. This is not an atomic cross-process protocol: another process could change the current database or sidecars after the checks, or continue writing the legacy database after the snapshot. It does not detect partially imported databases already left by an older build. Snapshot paths must be valid UTF-8 for SQLite; non-UTF-8 paths fail closed. Directory entries are not synchronously persisted, so sudden power loss is not covered. SQLite `VACUUM` can renumber implicit rowids in tables without an integer primary key; the application queries checked here do not depend on those rowids, but external consumers might. Windows and macOS runtime behavior, directory ACL inheritance, and read-only WAL permissions still require platform testing.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/linear-string-scans.md b/docs/decisions/linear-string-scans.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..4caef881
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/linear-string-scans.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Linear scans for long near-matches
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The onboarding tip router and OpenAI-compatible URL normalizer both stripped trailing slashes with an unanchored `\/+$` regex. A 100,000-slash run followed by a non-slash makes the engine retry the run at each offset. Both focused tests exceeded an external timeout before the change. They now use the same backward scanner; the component still maps an empty result to `/`, while the URL normalizer retains its prior empty result. The focused component and URL tests pass after the change.
+
+The fast-style spoken-domain frame lookup used an unanchored `\S+\s+$` regex. Its input is limited to a 15,000-character styling chunk, so the isolated regex's five-second timeout on 100,000 punctuation characters is not a measurement of application latency. The backward scanner avoids retrying suffixes within that bounded chunk. A generated comparison of 100,000 prefix strings, including Unicode whitespace and surrogate pairs, agreed with the old regex's extracted frame, and the fast-style suite passes. The cardinal-formatting regex SonarCloud also flagged only receives a JavaScript `number`'s bounded string representation, not an arbitrary transcript string.
+
+The same slash regex remains in `scripts/ci/release-shell-contracts.test.mjs`; it operates on a workflow line in a test rather than on an application URL. It was not changed solely to silence the warning.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/local-clear-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/local-clear-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..bcc5c7f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/local-clear-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# Local-data clear ownership
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The browser clear gate supported multiple owners, but the account-deletion and local-clear dialogs independently retired the same durable marker and invoked native completion. A failed account deletion could remove the marker after another clear started; a reload would then lose the browser-side recovery latch. The two destructive workflows now acquire exclusive clear ownership before waiting for active writes. A competing clear is rejected without touching the marker, and the localized clear dialog tells the user to retry. The shared gate still supports multiple nonexclusive owners for other callers; an abandoned account deletion only removes its marker if no other owner exists.
+
+A recovery marker is pending based on its presence, not its contents. Even an empty marker blocks writes and opens the retry dialog. This aligns the read with marker creation, which preserves any existing value.
+
+The browser wipe had two independent app-owned-key lists. The active clear missed `maus-settings-last-page` and other `maus-settings` keys even though the older helper covered them. Both paths now share one ownership predicate, including Mixpanel storage, and exclude the recovery marker until explicit completion. Firebase session keys and unrelated keys remain untouched.
+
+Tests reproduce the failed-deletion marker race, exercise exclusive ownership while writes drain and after retry, check empty-marker recovery, and verify that both storage paths preserve the marker and remove settings and analytics keys. These guards are per WebView process; native clear-marker and database quarantine crash-recovery limitations remain separately documented.
+
+If an account-deletion attempt fails before it acquires a clear lease, it must not retire any marker. A regression test covers an unavailable marker read while another clear owns the gate. When browser storage becomes unavailable after the native wipe, the active wipe now raises the typed storage error already handled by the dialog, so it describes the partial outcome and keeps recovery pending.
+
+The native pending marker is created exclusively and an existing regular marker is left untouched. A marker symlink or other non-regular entry is rejected instead of being followed or truncated; recovery still treats its presence as pending. This protects unrelated files from marker creation. As with the prior marker implementation, ordinary file creation is not a guarantee that a sudden power loss has durably persisted the directory entry.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/local-model-refresh-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/local-model-refresh-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..97a9ade6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/local-model-refresh-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# Local device and model-status refresh ownership
+
+Local device enumeration and model-status checks can overlap when settings are opened or the selected device changes. A late earlier result or failure previously replaced newer state, stopped its loading indicator, or saved a fallback device computed from a selection the user had since changed. Both refresh paths now have separate renderer-local request ownership and local-data-clear generation/marker checks. Device enumeration only writes a fallback if the selection is still the one it read. Model status results also require the local mode and CPU/GPU preference still to match the request. An obsolete failure does not clear current data or show an error.
+
+These checks do not cancel already-started sidecar work or coordinate other renderers. Settings-initiated downloads now reserve a per-model slot synchronously before sidecar startup, suppressing duplicate clicks until the original task settles, while unrelated models remain independent. A duplicate caller does not wait for or receive the first caller's outcome. The slot is released on success or failure. Pause, cancel, and delete are not serialized with download, however, and a resume requested before the prior task settles can be ignored. Automatic transcription downloads and other renderers do not share the settings reservation; facade ownership is described below. A stalled enumeration or preference write can leave loading pending; a clear-invalidated request deliberately avoids changing cleared state and can leave the loading flag set until another refresh or reset. Sidecar behavior and real hardware were mocked by the regression tests.
+
+Settings downloads now also refuse to start during a pending clear and ignore progress, completion and failure callbacks belonging to a generation before the clear. The reservation is still released when the task settles. This prevents stale callbacks from repopulating renderer state or showing a stale error, not the sidecar download itself: work already dispatched can continue on disk, and existing progress state is not actively cleared by this guard. Pause, cancel and delete now apply the same clear-start and completion guard, but their already-dispatched native effects still run. Their pre-clear UI flags can remain until reset, and they are not otherwise serialized with downloads. Automatic downloads and other renderers remain outside these guards.
+
+The settings actions display generic sidecar failure messages instead of interpolating arbitrary exceptions, trading low-level diagnostic detail for less accidental disclosure. Progress snapshots may themselves contain an error string, and other sidecar consumers and logs are not covered by this change.
+
+A failed settings cancel no longer deletes the displayed download snapshot as if cancellation had succeeded. A successful cancel removes only the job that was displayed when cancellation began, rather than erasing a newer job's snapshot. This is renderer-state ownership, not a job-specific native cancellation: the sidecar API cancels by model, and an uncertain failed response may have committed. Late progress for the old job and model-level cancellation races remain possible.
+
+Similarly, a delayed pause response cannot replace a different job's current snapshot. Pause still addresses a model rather than a specific job, and same-job progress/resume races or a new job not yet represented in the store are not fully coordinated.
+
+The facade now reserves a per-model runtime promise before asynchronous startup, so concurrent downloads and pause/cancel requests within the renderer select the same runtime. Active downloads hold that reservation through settlement, a paused result retains it for resume, and an eventual successful resume can release it. Failed cancel retains the owner; successful cancel requests release when active downloads finish. Readiness caches are invalidated in either outcome. This coordinates runtime choice, not the sidecar job lifecycle: pause or cancel can still reach the sidecar before its download job has been created, concurrent operations can affect the same model, and a lost response can have committed remotely. A retained paused owner can outlive a job, and other renderers do not share the reservation.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/lock-cargo-in-packaged-builds.md b/docs/decisions/lock-cargo-in-packaged-builds.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c30482d7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/lock-cargo-in-packaged-builds.md
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# Packaged builds must honor Cargo lockfiles
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+PR 317's Socket report flagged `web-sys` 0.3.107 with a supply-chain score of
+13. The repository's tracked Cargo lockfiles currently resolve `web-sys` to
+0.3.94 in the desktop and Windows installer workspaces and 0.3.104 in the
+transcription workspace. The patched CPAL manifest also declares `web-sys` for
+WebAssembly targets with a compatible version range. The report alone does not
+establish which path caused Socket to select 0.3.107.
+
+Socket's [0.3.107 alerts page](https://socket.dev/cargo/package/web-sys/alerts/0.3.107)
+lists install scripts, network, shell, environment and filesystem access for
+the package and its dependencies. The published
+[manifest](https://socket.dev/cargo/package/web-sys/files/0.3.107/web-sys-0.3.107/Cargo.toml)
+sets `build = false` for `web-sys` itself. This does not establish that its
+dependencies are safe or that every alert is a false positive. Socket also
+scores the locked [0.3.94](https://socket.dev/cargo/package/web-sys/overview/0.3.94)
+and [0.3.104](https://socket.dev/cargo/package/web-sys/overview/0.3.104) at
+13, so replacing 0.3.107 with either version would not resolve the reported
+risk by itself.
+
+The release and desktop build workflows tested Rust with `--locked`, but their
+Tauri build commands did not pass it to Cargo. The Windows native pill sidecar
+build also omitted it. A build could therefore resolve a different dependency
+graph from the one reviewed in the lockfile.
+
+## Decision
+
+The desktop CI and release workflows pass `-- --locked` to Tauri. The native
+Windows pill sidecar build passes `--locked` directly to Cargo. The transcription
+sidecar build already uses `--locked`. Tauri documents that arguments after
+`--` reach its build runner, and Cargo documents that `--locked` fails if the
+lockfile is absent or needs a different resolution.
+
+- [Tauri CLI build arguments](https://v2.tauri.app/reference/cli/)
+- [Cargo lockfile options](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/commands/cargo.html)
+
+## Consequences
+
+A stale or missing lockfile now fails these packaged builds instead of silently
+changing their dependency graph. A deliberate update still requires reviewing
+and committing the new lockfile. The regression guard in
+`scripts/ci/cargo-lock-builds.test.mjs` checks the workflow commands and the
+pill build arguments. Platform build jobs must still verify the real packaging
+path.
+
+This limits unreviewed resolution changes. It does not clear Socket's warning,
+prove the locked packages safe, cover standalone unlocked local builds, or
+replace a dependency audit. Investigate the report's exact alert locations and
+the dependency graph before accepting or dismissing the remaining risk.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/logger-structured-failures.md b/docs/decisions/logger-structured-failures.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c37e3c81
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/logger-structured-failures.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Structured logging failure paths
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The desktop logger previously fell back to `String(value)` if JSON serialization threw before structural masking. A custom `toString` could therefore return an unlabelled password. The unhandled-rejection handler likewise stringified object reasons before the logger could mask their fields. Regression tests reproduced both leaks. Failed object serialization now emits `[redaction-failed]`, and rejection reasons are passed as structured arguments. Primitive rejection reasons remain readable. Transcript-correction failures also pass the error as a separate logger argument instead of interpolating it first.
+
+Both the desktop masker and the shared utilities masker also avoid copying callable properties into sanitized output. A getter that only exposes `toJSON` on a later read otherwise lets serialization invoke a newly copied method after the masking pass; a focused logger test reproduced that leak.
+
+The URL logging helper also fails closed on an unparseable URL. Returning the original input on parse failure could expose a token or vocabulary query value precisely when the helper was unable to apply its requested masks.
+
+This does not make every diagnostic safe by construction. Plain string arguments are still passed through by the logger, and its `Error` stack scrubber recognizes prefixed token shapes rather than every labelled secret. Other call sites that interpolate sensitive objects before logging need separate review.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/logger-text-redaction.md b/docs/decisions/logger-text-redaction.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..adf01c08
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/logger-text-redaction.md
@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+# Logger text redaction boundary
+
+The native logger previously passed ordinary string arguments straight through, even though many call sites interpolate errors and credentials into strings. It now applies the shared sensitive-token redactor and the desktop provider-key pattern to adjacent free-form arguments, including error stacks. It also sanitizes string values inside the already masked structured-object representation before serializing, preserving valid JSON for structured logs. Adjacent strings are scrubbed together so an authorization scheme and credential split across arguments are not automatically exposed.
+
+Each free-form string group or structured string value is limited to 16,384 UTF-16 units before text redaction, avoiding a dangling surrogate and indicating truncation. This is not complete secret detection: arbitrary unlabelled tokens and secrets split across object/argument boundaries can still leak, and truncation can affect label recognition. Serializing or traversing a huge or hostile object can still require unbounded work before the text limit applies; redaction is not a sandbox for getters, proxies or custom serializers. Raw `console` calls and other logging implementations are outside this boundary. The token redactor can also hide benign text that resembles credentials, and long diagnostics are intentionally truncated.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/migration-069-crlf-digests.md b/docs/decisions/migration-069-crlf-digests.md
