index 00000000..fdd18aa3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/migration-069-crlf-digests.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Migration 069 historical line endings
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The migration ledger accepts two superseded, comment-only forms of migration 069. The raw SQL checksum written by sqlx differs when a checkout has CRLF line endings. Accepting only the LF digests can reject a database recorded from a CRLF copy of either historical form.
+
+The SQL at commits `0b1b342d126657c02d450ee42f38c64703bc89f9` and `f9747421e622f4b8d0b825cb98c9a2f1d306873b` has the two existing LF digests. Folding each file to LF and expanding every LF to CRLF produces the two new SHA-384 digests in `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/db/open.rs`. The fixed entries do not accept arbitrary migration edits. They retain the existing limitation that a later edit to the current 069 SQL is not independently checked when a database carries a superseded digest.
+
+The commits inspected here already contain the SQL line-ending attribute. That does not prove an ordinary checkout of either commit produced CRLF. The added entries cover historical builds or checkouts that did record these exact CRLF variants; the number of affected installations is unknown.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/model-migration-race.md b/docs/decisions/model-migration-race.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..9456a16e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/model-migration-race.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Legacy model migration publication race
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+`migrate_model_files` used to check whether a destination existed and then call `rename`, with a `copy` fallback. The check did not reserve the destination. A local Linux reproduction placed a newly downloaded file at the destination after the check but before the rename; `rename` replaced it with the legacy file and removed the legacy name. The copy fallback could also overwrite a destination created after the check.
+
+Migration now first attempts an atomic no-replace hard link, followed by removal of the old name. If linking is unavailable, it copies into a uniquely named temporary file in the destination directory, synchronizes the copied file, and uses `tempfile`'s no-clobber publication before removing the source. A destination that wins the race is preserved, as is the source. Ordinary copy and publication failures attempt to clean up the temporary file; failures never intentionally publish a partial copy. Existing destination entries, including links and directories, are left untouched.
+
+The fallback can require additional space and time, and it fails rather than overwriting if the filesystem cannot support no-clobber publication. Process crashes can leave hidden `.maus-model-migration-*` staging files: automatic cleanup is intentionally not attempted because another process may still be copying. Directory entries are not synchronously persisted, so this is not a power-loss durability guarantee. The migration assumes legacy model files are not concurrently replaced or modified. Regression tests exercise both publication paths, collisions and an injected mid-copy failure on Linux; Windows and macOS still need CI coverage.
