+they were cheap enough to build: the second delivery ships them, and the fourth adds
+Chromium (G25), so a recording can now run on Linux x86-64.
 
 ## Consequences
 
diff --git a/docs/decisions/ollama-picker-probe-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/ollama-picker-probe-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..8b1d34f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/ollama-picker-probe-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+# Ollama picker probe ownership
+
+The Ollama model picker now uses a distinct component owner for each endpoint and credential, so a previous model list cannot flash or remain selectable while a new configuration is probed, including when switching back to a prior configuration. Availability is published only after the model list succeeds. Failure logging no longer includes arbitrary endpoint exception text, and cancellation of an obsolete owner is not logged as an active failure.
+
+Each of the availability and listing operations retains its own ten-second deadline and abort signal. Abort is cooperative and cannot recall an already-dispatched request. A retired `withTimeout` race can keep its timer until it settles or times out even after unmount; its eventual result is ignored. Polling still retries while unavailable. Tests mock the repository, not a live endpoint, and other settings or provider error paths are not covered by this change.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/openai-compatible-probe-deadline.md b/docs/decisions/openai-compatible-probe-deadline.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..ba25ef51
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/openai-compatible-probe-deadline.md
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# OpenAI-compatible model probe deadline
+
+The settings picker probes availability and then lists models in one logical run. Its deadline now covers both awaits: a server that answers the first request but stalls on the second no longer leaves the picker waiting indefinitely. On timeout the controller is aborted, manual entry is enabled, and a later retry owns the UI; late results from the abandoned run cannot publish. Failures log a generic diagnostic rather than an endpoint error that might contain a credential.
+
+Abort is cooperative: a transport that ignores the signal may keep underlying work alive even though the UI retires the run. Retries continue while the picker remains mounted and the endpoint is unavailable. The deadline applies to the combined probe, so a slow availability check leaves less time for listing. Tests use mocked requests, not real network endpoints.
+
+The picker also gives each endpoint, key and path configuration a fresh fetched-state owner and waits for listing to complete before enabling model selection. Manual-input preference remains shared across configuration changes. A previous configuration's in-flight callbacks are ignored after unmount; the transport cancellation limit above still applies.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/openrouter-model-load-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/openrouter-model-load-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..aebd57e2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/openrouter-model-load-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# OpenRouter model-load ownership
+
+Model catalog requests could complete out of order, overwrite a newer credential's results, or repopulate the shared list after an explicit cache clear or local-data clear. Each request now has a renderer-local sequence and clear generation, checks the shared clear marker, and verifies the selected key and its credential before publishing either success or error. Explicit cache clearing invalidates outstanding requests. An expanded picker clears and reloads the shared catalog when the selected post-processing key or its credential changes.
+
+The catalog is still a single shared list, fetched with the selected post-processing key even when a different OpenRouter card is expanded. Simultaneously expanded cards can initiate duplicate requests; only the latest response publishes. A collapsed picker may temporarily display a label from the previously cached shared catalog until opened. Clear-invalidated reads can leave the status loading until a later reset or request. The sequence and generation are renderer-local, and a different renderer's clear that both starts and finishes between marker checks cannot be observed. Network calls already sent cannot be cancelled and still hold the credential until they settle; no provider or real WebView was used in these tests.
+
+Provider-routing and favorite updates now derive their nested config from the latest key inside the existing per-key write lane. Overlapping toggles and routing/favorite updates therefore compose within one renderer, and the UI consumes rejected save promises after the API-key action reports the failure. A failed API-key update is treated as an uncertain commit: the next derived config update rereads the key in the same lane, declines to recreate a missing key, and refuses to save if reconciliation fails. Ordinary direct partial updates do not perform that pre-read; a successful direct update's returned full row clears the reconciliation requirement. Other renderers and direct database writes are not coordinated, and routing controls still submit whole routing objects, so stale UI actions can overwrite other routing fields.
+
+Failed model requests now log only a generic failure rather than the raw provider or transport exception, which may contain the credential or response content. This intentionally gives up detailed diagnostics for this path; other API-key and provider error paths have not been established to provide the same guarantee.
+
+The separate public provider catalog also uses latest-request and local-clear ownership checks, and logs generic failures. Duplicate concurrent provider requests are still possible. As with the model catalog, a read invalidated by a clear can leave the status loading until reset, and a clear entirely completed in another renderer between checks cannot be detected. The endpoint requires no API key, but arbitrary server error text is still withheld.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/paste-review-concurrency.md b/docs/decisions/paste-review-concurrency.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..feef1598
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/paste-review-concurrency.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Pending paste review concurrency
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+A pending paste review has two writes around a clipboard operation: save the edited text, copy it, then mark it copied. Same-tick clicks could launch Copy and Cancel before React rendered the disabled controls, and callers from separate mounted cards could overlap even after a component-level guard. The card now uses a synchronous ref guard; the action layer also excludes overlapping operations on the same review within one renderer. Failed operations release both guards so the user can retry, and unrelated reviews remain independent.
+
+A copy now holds one local-data write lease across both saves and the clipboard operation. It checks for a clear request before starting the clipboard write and after it finishes. A clear waits for an already-started operation, but an in-flight native clipboard write cannot be cancelled; local-data clear does not promise to erase existing system clipboard contents. If clear starts after copying begins, the copy may have reached the clipboard even though the action reports interruption.
+
+These protections do not coordinate separate renderer processes or prevent a stale message object from being submitted after an earlier operation has finished. The database update is not a compare-and-swap, so other mutation paths can still race. If copying succeeds but the final database update fails, the review can remain pending although the clipboard changed; no exactly-once clipboard guarantee is made.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/preference-failure-disclosure.md b/docs/decisions/preference-failure-disclosure.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..7ce2c274
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/preference-failure-disclosure.md
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# Preference repository failure disclosure
+
+Preferences may contain opaque tokens as well as URLs and other private values. A repository exception can echo those values even when they do not match a known credential pattern. The shared preference update path therefore reports generic diagnostics and rejects with a generic error for initial reads, writes, recovery reads, and reconciliation reads. Preference refresh and the Windows elevation preflight also avoid logging raw preference-read errors. Caller-supplied snackbar messages remain in use. A failed write still triggers reconciliation and prevents subsequent writes from using an unverified snapshot.
+
+This intentionally sacrifices low-level failure details. Lease acquisition errors and exceptions thrown by caller-provided update callbacks are outside these repository catches; callers must not put secrets in their own messages. Other profile, preference loading, native and provider paths are not covered by this change. Tests use mocked repositories and do not establish native IPC behavior.
+
+The shared profile update queue follows the same policy for repository save, recovery and reconciliation failures, and the ordinary profile refresh does not log repository errors. Its missing-user and lease errors retain their existing distinct messages. Usage metering and other profile operations are separate paths and may still expose details.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/provider-logos-are-brand-colour-not-lucide-glyphs.md b/docs/decisions/provider-logos-are-brand-colour-not-lucide-glyphs.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..95bb54d7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/provider-logos-are-brand-colour-not-lucide-glyphs.md
@@ -0,0 +1,99 @@
+# Provider marks are the brands' own colour artwork, not recoloured lucide glyphs
+
+Date: 2026-11-09
+Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The settings surface names providers in a dozen places — the API-key provider
+select, the "Provider: …" caption on an edit card, the key card's caption, the
+Overview's model rows, the two personal-key dialogs, and the disclosure sentence
+that says where dictation audio goes — and every one of them was text only.
+Nothing visually distinguished Groq from Deepgram, so the provider a row was
+about had to be read, not seen.
+
+`apps/desktop/DESIGN.md` says lucide (stroke 1.9) is the only icon family, so the
+first pass drew each provider the way the app draws everything else: a monochrome
+lucide-style glyph in `currentColor`, with the provider's initial as a fallback.
+That was rejected. A hand-drawn approximation of someone else's trademark is both
+a worse identifier than the real mark and a legal question nobody here is
+qualified to answer; and an initial in a circle is the generic-icon pattern the
+`antislop-ui` skill exists to refuse.
+
+## Decision
+
+Use each provider's **own published mark, in the colours it publishes**, at
+identity size next to the provider's name.
+
+Vendored artwork. The marks live in
+`apps/desktop/src/assets/provider-logos/`, normalised to a transparent SVG with
+an explicit `viewBox`, and generated into a single committed React module
+(`ProviderLogos.tsx`) by `pnpm --filter desktop run gen:provider-logos`. The
+Tauri CSP (`img-src 'self' asset: https://asset.localhost data: blob:`) forbids
+hot-linking, and a remote fetch would also tell a third party that the user
+opened settings — which contradicts the app's own privacy stance.
+
+Three kinds of mark, one rule each:
+
+| Kind | Providers | Rule |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Colour | AssemblyAI, Azure, Cerebras, Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, Groq, OpenRouter | drawn exactly as published, one file for both schemes |
+| Ink | Deepgram, ElevenLabs, Ollama, OpenAI, xAI | published as one colour only, so they are drawn in `currentColor` and follow the surrounding text ink |
+| Per-scheme pair | Gladia (navy / white), OpenRouter on onyx | one file per surface, because one file cannot clear the contrast floor on both |
+
+A mark is never recoloured, never tinted with the app palette, never inverted,
+never placed on a coloured chip, outlined, rotated or stretched. The tile behind
+it is a plain flex box of exactly the mark's size; the only scheme-dependent
+decision is *which* file to draw.
+
+Providers with no verifiable published mark — Aldea, Speaches, OpenAI
+compatible — render **nothing**. No stand-in glyph, no initial: the provider's
+name beside the slot is the identifier, and a generic icon there would only say
+"provider", which the row already says.
+
+Rejected:
+
+- **Monochrome lucide-style glyphs for every provider.** It is a redraw of a
+  trademark we do not own, it makes every provider look like the app's own icon
+  set, and it throws away the one thing a brand mark is for.
+- **Letter/initial fallbacks.** The generic-icon pattern; also unreadable at
+  14–16px.
+- **The `?react` svgr import the desktop app already has.** `apps/preview` has no
+  svgr and imports the real desktop components, so `?react` would typecheck in
+  the app and break the design site. A generated plain-React module works in
+  both bundles with no per-app plugin and no `.d.ts` shim.
+- **`@iconify-json/logos` wordmarks.** They are horizontal lockups (512×58 to
+  512×266) and are unreadable as 16–22px identity marks.
+- **A logo CDN.** Brandfetch's picker is hotlink-only with 24-hour URLs, which is
+  both a CSP violation and a runtime dependency on a third party for a desktop
+  app that must work offline.
+- **Tinting a failing mark into the palette.** Recolouring a trademark to make it
+  legible is exactly what the brand guidelines forbid; the answer is a different
+  *published* rendering, which is what the per-scheme pairs are.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Each provider is now identifiable at a glance, and the identity is the brand's
+own — no redraw, no approximation, no invented colour.
+
+The cost is that colour is not ours to fix. A mark whose only published colour
+sinks into one of the two surfaces has to fall back to the brand's own
+mono/reversed rendering, so two providers carry a second file and the component
+holds a small per-provider map. That map is the thing to check when a provider is
+added: `ProviderLogo.test.tsx` pins the three providers that render nothing, and
+measures every colour mark against the app's real surfaces (cream `#FDFBF8`,
+onyx `#161617`) at the 3:1 floor for a non-text graphic.
+
+Two further consequences worth naming:
+
+1. The artwork is a **third-party asset set** with a licensing story. Provenance
+   for every file, and the trademark notice, are in
+   `apps/desktop/src/assets/provider-logos/README.md`. The drawings come from
+   CC0 (`@iconify-json/logos`) and MIT (`@lobehub/icons-static-svg`) icon sets and
+   one upstream repository (Gladia's own `gladiaflow`); the *marks* remain
+   trademarks of their owners and this project claims no endorsement.
+2. `DESIGN.md`'s "lucide is the only icon family" still governs chrome glyphs.
+   This decision carves out **third-party identity only**: a mark identifies
+   someone else's product, a glyph describes our own UI. The two never mix — a
+   provider mark is never used as a control icon, and lucide is never used to
+   stand in for a brand.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/quarantine-recovery-limits.md b/docs/decisions/quarantine-recovery-limits.md
