index 00000000..2d7d0bd3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/quarantine-recovery-limits.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Database quarantine recovery limits
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+`apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/db/open.rs` moves the database and SQLite sidecars into a quarantine directory before creating a replacement database. If a later move fails and a rollback move also fails, the error names both the original failure and each file that could not be restored; remaining rollback moves are still attempted.
+
+New quarantines create an in-progress marker before moving any file. A later normal startup seeing that marker fails closed before opening the database or importing a legacy copy, because either the database or a sidecar may be stranded. This is conservative: even a failure before any move, or a successful rollback after a move failure, can require manual inspection and removal of the marker. Once all moves succeed, a history marker prevents a later missing current database from silently resurrecting the pre-rebrand database. An already-pending local-data clear bypasses the quarantine import guard so its recovery can proceed without importing the old copy. The clear removes the markers after deleting the quarantined files. Tests exercise stranded rollback, preservation of an existing main file, recovery bypass, completed quarantine, and marker symlinks.
+
+These markers are synchronized as files, but their parent directory is not synchronously persisted; they are not a power-loss durability guarantee. They do not coordinate different processes, and historical quarantines created before this marker existed cannot be identified reliably from a name alone. A clear recovery may still be unable to reach its dialog if opening the remaining SQLite files itself fails. No automatic reconstruction of interrupted quarantines is attempted.
+
+SQLite can return `malformed database schema` while parsing a schema entry, including when the entry's SQL cannot be prepared by the current SQLite build. The current classifier does not quarantine that message. Automatically treating every such error as physical damage could replace a database that a compatible build can still read. This case needs compatibility fixtures before expanding the quarantine rule.
+
+The privacy wipe still identifies old quarantine entries by a numeric name suffix, which does not prove ownership. Cleanup now opens the parent and archive directories through held no-follow capabilities, rejects linked or special entries, and validates all archive contents before removing only known database and SQLite sidecar names. Unexpected contents fail the wipe instead of being recursively deleted; the native clear marker remains pending. Standalone numeric files are still removed for compatibility with older backups. A coincidentally named user directory containing only recognized names, or a coincidentally named standalone file, remains indistinguishable and may be removed. This approach does not coordinate hostile concurrent filesystem mutation, and Windows reparse-point behavior requires platform testing.
