index 00000000..5cdb504c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/release-artifact-completeness.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Release artifact completeness
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The release matrix builds macOS, Windows and Linux, but a successful job could upload no files and the manifest builder accepted any nonempty subset of signed targets. A published release could therefore omit an updater target while becoming the latest release for everyone. Uploads now fail when a matrix job finds no files, and the release workflow opts into a full manifest gate requiring the macOS application and DMG, Windows MSI and NSIS, and Linux AppImage updater targets, plus the MSI, EXE and DEB manual installers. The builder retains its partial-manifest mode for standalone use and targeted fixtures.
+
+GitHub release assets are flattened to basenames. Two uploaded files with the same name can otherwise overwrite one another even when only one was verified against its signature. Full release builds reject duplicate asset names. The manifest builder also rejects multiple signed candidates for one updater target rather than selecting one based on filesystem traversal order.
+
+An explicit tag is checked with Git's ref-format validator before it reaches workflow output files. `beta-channel` is reserved for the rolling beta manifest. Release download URLs encode tags as a single path segment, including when rendering the Ruby Homebrew cask. This does not solve the separate existing-tag and tag-creation race documented in `release-event-sha.md`.
+
+Tests exercise each missing target and installer, duplicate basenames, ambiguous signed candidates, invalid tags and encoded cask tags. They use synthetic artifacts; the real release remains dependent on the platform build jobs, Tauri's emitted filenames, signing keys and GitHub's upload service.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/release-event-sha.md b/docs/decisions/release-event-sha.md
