@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+# Remote device dialog and receiver status
+
+The manual pairing dialog takes a synchronous per-dialog save guard before awaiting persistence, disables its fields and buttons while saving, and refuses close requests until completion. A failed save releases the guard for retry. Before saving an added device or a changed device ID, it checks the current renderer's device map for an existing ID and presents an inline error rather than knowingly overwriting that device's secret. Editing the same ID remains allowed. The invite dialog likewise guards same-tick duplicate pairing requests synchronously, disables its input, and prevents closing while the request is pending.
+
+The receiver-port form and preference setter reject non-integers and values outside the TCP port range 1–65535 before saving or restarting the receiver. A blank value still requests automatic assignment. This avoids persisting values that cannot deserialize to the native `u16` port and then stopping a working receiver before restart fails. Existing invalid preferences are not migrated. A synchronous UI guard prevents overlapping toggles and port applies, and disables the port input during the operation. After saving a port, restart proceeds only if the receiver is still enabled and no later renderer start/stop intent has superseded it; a later intent during the stop prevents the follow-up start. A clear generation check also suppresses the remaining steps after a clear starts, even if it finishes before the await resumes.
+
+Receiver status polling records request order and start/stop generations. A successful poll updates the renderer only if it has not been overtaken by a newer successful poll or spanned a start/stop, and no start/stop is currently active. Failed newer polls do not suppress earlier successful polls. The method still returns the raw response to its caller, even when publication to state is suppressed. An operation failure invalidates polls that spanned it, but a subsequent poll can reconcile status.
+
+Startup takes receiver-command, receiver-preference and clear-generation snapshots before loading. After reconciliation it reads current preferences and starts only if those snapshots are still current and no receiver command or relevant preference mutation is in flight. Preference setters register intent before their queued save, so a pending disable or port edit cannot be missed. Conflicting startup is skipped rather than automatically retried; a later initialization may try again.
+
+Selecting the first receiver already enables remote output in the same preference mutation; the dialog no longer follows that selection with a redundant enable that could override a newer disable. Its test-send button has a synchronous guard against duplicate same-tick sends and releases it on failure.
+
+Component tests reproduced duplicate same-tick manual saves, invite submissions, port applies and test sends, an ID collision, a stale port apply restarting a stopped receiver, and a redundant sender enable overriding a newer disable. They exercise disabled controls, Escape, retries, save-before-retire ordering, superseding receiver commands and clear transitions with mocked actions. Deferred action tests reproduced polling responses overwriting newer starts and polls, startup overriding an explicit stop or a preference change, and startup using a preference whose disable save was pending. They also cover clear generation, overlapping startup, fresh preferences and mutation failures. Translations for the collision error are authored in the override catalog.
+
+Native receiver start now captures the disabled receiver generation before binding and atomically checks it when publishing the listener. A stop or competing start invalidates the token, so an in-flight start cannot re-enable the receiver after a stop. A superseded start returns an error; the uncommitted listener is dropped. State-level Rust tests reproduce the previous start/stop interleaving and exercise competing starts and stop/restart. They do not simulate a real socket bind or a full Tauri invocation.
+
+The dialog guards are renderer-local, not a database uniqueness transaction or cross-window locks. The collision check can race another writer between the in-memory check and native upsert; a stale or incomplete map can also miss an existing ID. Two different dialogs, native pairing, or another process can still modify the same pairing concurrently. Existing writes cannot be cancelled by closing or clearing the UI. Poll request ordering is not a native timestamp, and the returned raw status may be stale. Native receiver generation coordination applies only to callers sharing that process's receiver state, not separate app processes. The startup and port-change intent guards apply only to this renderer and to the tracked actions; direct native or other-process changes can still race them. A port save and receiver stop/start are not an atomic transaction, and a failure can leave the preference saved while the receiver is stopped.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/remote-lan-security.md b/docs/decisions/remote-lan-security.md
