index 00000000..778d5961
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/remote-lan-security.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Multi-device LAN security boundary
+
+The current LAN protocol is newline-delimited JSON over raw TCP. Pairing requests transmit the pairing code, pairing responses transmit the shared secret, session hello transmits that secret as an authentication token, and final-text messages transmit transcripts. None is encrypted in transit. In particular, the sender sends the secret before checking the receiver's claimed device ID, and that ID is not cryptographic proof of the peer. A network observer or an endpoint impersonating the receiver can obtain credentials and text; a party with a shared secret can impersonate a sender. The settings dialog now warns that this traffic is unencrypted and should only be used on a trusted network. This warning is a mitigation, not a protocol security fix.
+
+An authenticated, encrypted protocol with explicit peer verification, replay protection, a versioned rollout and compatibility handling needs separate design and testing with all peer implementations. This change does not claim transport confidentiality or authenticated receiver identity. Invite codes themselves contain sensitive pairing information and must be shared securely.
+
+Both peers now limit each JSON frame to 4 MiB of UTF-8 bytes, excluding its newline. Oversized outbound messages fail locally and oversized inbound frames close the connection; this is a protocol compatibility limit for older or other peers. The receiver still accepts connections from all interfaces and starts a task per connection without a connection cap or idle timeout. Many idle connections can consume tasks and descriptors; a bounded frame is not a complete availability defense.
+
+Pairing now atomically consumes a matching invite for the active receiver generation before storing a sender. Concurrent requests cannot both use that code. If persistence or response delivery fails after consumption, the invite is still invalidated and a new code must be used; a sender may have been stored even if the response was lost. This does not protect an observed code from being raced or guessed. No live adversarial network or cross-platform interoperability test has been performed.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/remote-receiver-reauthorizes-every-frame.md b/docs/decisions/remote-receiver-reauthorizes-every-frame.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..125db692
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/remote-receiver-reauthorizes-every-frame.md
@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# The remote receiver re-authorizes every frame against the device row
+
+Date: 2026-11-06
+Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+A paired phone delivers final transcripts to the desktop over plain TCP on the
+LAN. The receiver used to compare the pairing code once at `SessionHello`, keep
+the accepted device id in memory, and trust that connection for its lifetime.
+Two things cannot be expressed in that shape: revoking a device while it holds an
+open connection, and noticing that the connection no longer belongs to a trusted
+device at all.
+
+The transport has no TLS, so the shared secret is a bearer token and the receiver
+is the only thing that bounds what a peer may do with it.
+
+## Decision
+
+Pairing stays a one-time exchange, and the accepted device receives a 16-byte
+shared secret. `SessionHello` verifies it with `secret_matches`, which compares
+the full secret instead of stopping at the first differing byte, and the session
+keeps the token rather than only the device id.
+
+Frames are newline delimited, and `read_frame` refuses a frame larger than four
+mebibytes. The cap exists because authorization happens after parsing: a peer
+that never sends a newline could otherwise grow the read buffer until the
+process ran out of memory, and nothing has checked that peer's secret yet.
+
+### Framing correction, 2026-11-08
+
+The four-mebibyte limit includes the newline. The receiver rejects EOF with a
+nonempty, unterminated frame even when those bytes form valid JSON. It checks
+the remaining capacity before copying input into the frame buffer. A clean EOF
+between frames still closes the connection normally.
+
+After that, every `final_text` frame is authorized on its own.
+`authorize_final_text` re-reads the device row inside the same generation guard
+the connection already uses and requires it to still be trusted and to still
+match the retained secret. A failed check clears the session, so the peer has to
+present `SessionHello` again before it can deliver.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Revoking or re-trusting a device takes effect on its next frame, with no
+connection bookkeeping to keep in sync, at the cost of one indexed SQLite read
+per delivery frame.
+
+Because there is no TLS, a peer that can read the LAN traffic learns the secret
+and can impersonate the sender until the device is revoked. The receiver accepts
+that on a trusted home network and mitigates it with single-use pairing and
+per-frame authorization and a bounded frame.
+
+The receiver keeps no read or write timeout, and it does not cap the number of
+open connections: a peer that connects and stays silent holds a task until it
+sends a frame or disconnects. Both were considered and left out of this change,
+which is why the framing cap is the only bound on a single peer.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/remote-target-concurrency.md b/docs/decisions/remote-target-concurrency.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..19b78190
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/remote-target-concurrency.md
@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Remote target preference concurrency
+
+Paired-device lookups require an own property, so inherited names such as `toString` are not treated as real receivers. Registration builds a new map using own computed properties, preserving IDs such as `__proto__` without changing the map's prototype. Tests cover both repository loads and invite registration, plus reconciliation and deletion.
+
+Device deletion checks the current target in the serialized preference mutation, after repository deletion succeeds. It clears the target and disables remote output only if that device is still selected. A queued selection checks the in-memory device map when its mutation executes; a selection of an absent device becomes a no-op. If its save has already begun, deletion's later queued cleanup clears it. Startup reconciliation checks the latest target and device map within one queued mutation, rather than clearing a target and disabling output in two writes based on an earlier snapshot.
+
+For an ID edit, the dialog persists the replacement first, then retires the old row. Retirement re-points the target only if it still names the old row, preserving the current output-enabled preference in that same mutation. If the replacement is no longer in the map, it clears the old target and disables output instead. Re-pairing no longer follows its selection write with a redundant enable write that can override a later disable.
+
+A successful device-list load replaces stale entries instead of merging them. If a later load starts, or the in-memory map changes during the read, the older result is discarded in its entirety; startup skips missing-target reconciliation for a discarded result. This avoids resurrecting deleted rows, including add-then-delete changes, but does not automatically retry a discarded read.
+
+Deferred repository and preference-save tests exercise stale deletion and startup cleanup, selection during deletion, queued and already-started selection saves, concurrent ID retirement and output changes, missing replacement, failed deletion, re-pairing followed by disable, stale list entries, delayed loads, add-then-delete, overlapping loads and failed reads. A dialog test checks that the replacement is persisted before retirement is invoked. These tests use mocked repositories rather than native pairing.
+
+These guarantees are per renderer and rely on the in-memory map and preference queue; they do not provide cross-process atomicity. Concurrent upserts/deletes of the same ID are not serialized. A discarded load can leave the device list incomplete or stale until a later load; external changes after a successful load are not observed automatically. Selection of an absent ID is silently ignored, and preference persistence failure after successful deletion can leave a durable dangling target until later reconciliation. An ID edit can leave both rows if deletion fails, or a deleted old row and dangling preference if the preference write fails. Its upsert and delete are not a database transaction. Pairing still selects its device after the native pairing request completes, potentially superseding a selection made during that request. Startup's receiver auto-start decision also uses an earlier preference snapshot. These need separate coordination and UX decisions.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/secret-scan-trust-boundaries.md b/docs/decisions/secret-scan-trust-boundaries.md
