index 00000000..23798e98
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/secret-scan-trust-boundaries.md
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# Secret scan trust boundaries
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The open review concerns in `.github/workflows/secret-scan.yml` describe distinct limits:
+
+- Pull requests from the same repository retain the proposed checkout's `.gitleaksignore`, which can suppress a finding in that pull request. The workflow warns about this; it does not prevent it. Fork pull requests strip that file. Stripping it for every pull request would also uncover the known historical test-fixture finding whose fingerprint is currently supplied only by the branch. That false positive needs a trusted-base disposition before a fail-closed change can land without blocking the existing consolidation scan.
+- Scans use the base policy, so a custom rule first introduced by a pull request is tested against a fixture but is not used to scan that pull request's history. The warning about an absent updater-key rule does not guarantee that an updater key in that range is detected. The rule must be deployed to the trusted base or supplied by a separately trusted policy before the scan can guarantee that coverage.
+- A `pull_request` workflow is itself read from the proposed merge commit. A contributor able to modify the workflow can change the check. A truly independent enforcement boundary requires repository-level rules or an appropriately isolated trusted workflow; running untrusted checkout code with `pull_request_target` privileges would create a different security risk.
+- On a push to `main`, the checkout used as policy is `main` after the push. It does not independently guard against a policy weakened in that push. Using the push's previous SHA needs explicit treatment for new branches, force pushes, unavailable commits and policy bootstrapping.
+
+These are not claims that a particular key is present. They are limits on what a green scan proves. Live add-then-delete and clean-tree controls passed with the pinned scanner, but those controls do not establish the trust guarantees above.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/session-paste-and-clipboard.md b/docs/decisions/session-paste-and-clipboard.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..c5b2f9c2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/session-paste-and-clipboard.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Session paste and clipboard outcomes
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+Firebase session paste acknowledgement previously cleared the session's paste fields unconditionally after asynchronous local delivery. If a newer paste arrived before the first delivery finished, the old acknowledgement erased it. The acknowledgement now uses a transaction on the session object, clearing only when both the timestamp and text still match the handled event. The transaction callback rechecks the local-clear generation on every retry and refuses to recreate a removed session. Component regressions simulate an overwritten message, unchanged message, and a retry after clear.
+
+A session remains a single remote slot, not a durable queue: a second sender write can overwrite an earlier message before the receiver observes it, timestamp collisions and clock skew are not solved, and a failed delivery is not guaranteed an immediate retry. Firebase transactions require appropriate read and write rules; these tests use a local mock, not the Firebase emulator or a live service. A clear cannot retract a network write already accepted by the server; existing session-removal coordination still applies.
+
+For local dictation, a clipboard fallback is successful handoff, but it is not insertion into the focused field. It now retires the prior edit watch without starting one against an unrelated field. Remote transcript feedback likewise reports a clipboard fallback rather than claiming insertion. The LAN receiver's delivery acknowledgement is still emitted when the native event is dispatched, before the frontend confirms paste or clipboard delivery; these changes do not provide end-to-end delivery acknowledgement.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/settings-load-ownership.md b/docs/decisions/settings-load-ownership.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..f8176b88
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/settings-load-ownership.md
@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Settings-data load ownership
+
+API-key reads, dictionary reads and style reads could publish rows captured before a local-data clear. A key or style read could also overwrite a newer local save or restore a deleted row. API-key callers that arrived while the first load was marked `loading` returned immediately rather than awaiting it, and creating a key in an unloaded store incorrectly marked the whole list loaded. A failed key load was swallowed, so personal-use setup could proceed as though the list were empty.
+
+API-key loads now share one pending promise per clear generation, wait for tracked local key mutations and retry an overlapping read. Clear invalidation rejects the old operation without displaying a stale error, and a new generation can start independently. List failures reject after the action records and reports the error; startup and settings retry handlers consume those failures, while personal-use setup stops instead of creating a key from an unknown list. A create no longer marks an otherwise unloaded list complete. Dictionary and style loads reject stale publication after a clear and let the latest load win; styles additionally track local saves/deletes so an overlapping list is retried.
+
+API-key and style writes for the same ID now run in submission order within this renderer, with a local-data write lease held while queued. A pending delete is shared by duplicate callers and rejects overlapping saves for that ID; other IDs can proceed independently. A failed operation does not poison its lane. Key deletion clears only preference references that still name the deleted ID when the preferences queue runs, including assistant selection, and preserves local or gateway modes when an inactive key reference is removed.
+
+These checks are scoped to tracked operations in one renderer, not cross-process database writes. An explicit create or style upsert submitted after deletion has finished can still recreate that ID; the guard does not provide durable tombstones or reject stale drafts across renderers. A key deletion can succeed before its preference update fails; the operation reports failure, but cannot undo the deletion. These list hydrations register returned rows and do not generally reconcile arbitrary rows removed externally. A clear cancelled before state reset can leave an old key status displaying `loading` until another load is requested; subsequent calls are allowed to retry. Reads are not cancelled at the native boundary and may retain their returned data until the promise settles; this is not a memory-zeroization guarantee. Retries can wait indefinitely on a stuck mutation, and tests use mocked repositories rather than a real database or multiple renderers.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/sherpa-cache-integrity.md b/docs/decisions/sherpa-cache-integrity.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..2eb0ad40
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/sherpa-cache-integrity.md
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# Sherpa extracted-cache integrity
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The extracted-tree walker discarded individual `read_dir` entry errors while calculating the cache digest. A directory walk could therefore finish with a digest of an incomplete view. It now propagates the entry error, so verification and recording fail closed instead. A synthetic iterator regression failed with the old filtering behavior and exercises the same collection helper used by the walker; the build-script test harness pins the test by name.
+
+This does not make the cache digest an adversarial local-writer boundary. The digest file is writable alongside the tree, and the existing walker intentionally does not hash symlinks or follow them. Added symlinks can therefore leave the digest unchanged, and the linker can follow them. Hashing link names alone would not validate outside targets; safely covering them requires a cross-platform policy for official archive symlinks and target resolution, including races, before changing cache compatibility. See the build script's existing comments for the trust and framing limits.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/skill-hash-limits.md b/docs/decisions/skill-hash-limits.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..074f4852
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/decisions/skill-hash-limits.md
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+# Vendored skill hash limits
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The skill verifier uses the upstream lock format: sorted relative file paths followed immediately by file bytes. This format has no representation for symbolic links or special files, so the verifier now rejects those entries, including a linked skill root, rather than silently ignoring them. Existing regular-file hashes remain unchanged.
+
+This is an integrity check against the lock file, not an independent trust anchor. The upstream format does not length-frame paths and contents, so different trees can have the same concatenated input even without a SHA-256 collision; empty directories also do not affect the digest. A format change would require coordination with the lock-file writer; this check must not be treated as proof against an adversarially constructed tree. It also does not coordinate against concurrent filesystem mutation, and Windows junction/reparse-point behavior has not been tested here. Project-owned unrecorded skills remain outside the locked-tree check.
