@@ -0,0 +1,9 @@
+# Updater fallback and resource ownership
+
+Date: 2026-11-08
+
+The manual macOS installer URL derives its release tag from a URL in the updater manifest. That path segment is already percent encoded; it is decoded before the URL builder encodes it, avoiding double encoding of tags with characters such as `+`, `#`, `%` or `/`. Malformed encoded segments are ignored. This is covered by local URL construction tests, not a live GitHub download.
+
+An installation now reports which path actually succeeded. A built-in update that falls back to opening a manual DMG must not relaunch the old app as if the built-in installation had succeeded. Successful built-in installations still relaunch. Once the manual installer is opened and the update handle is retired, the completion message stays visible but the app no longer treats that external step as a permanently active installation: update checks and channel changes can resume. They remain blocked while the download or resource retirement is actually in progress. If a built-in update succeeds but automatic relaunch fails, the dialog tells the user to restart manually and no longer leaves the app indefinitely busy.
+
+The beta-channel native command registers an update resource before its metadata reaches JavaScript. If decoding that metadata fails, JavaScript now attempts to close the newly registered handle without masking the original failure. A close failure is logged and can still leave a native resource alive; the tests use mocked Tauri resources rather than a real application process. These changes do not establish cross-process coordination or verify real macOS installer behavior.
diff --git a/docs/decisions/updater-trust-anchor.md b/docs/decisions/updater-trust-anchor.md
